.TH PIP 5
.SH NAME
pip \- the Osprey session protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #O/pip
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Pip is the protocol Osprey containers are spoken to with.  What it keeps
from 9P: authentication is someone else's business, fids name what a
conversation is working on, and every T message is answered by an R
message carrying its tag.  What it changes is three things.
.PP
A
.I session
outlives the connection that carried it.  It is named by a number, and a
client whose connection has gone opens another and says which session it
is resuming; its fids are still there.
.PP
Transactions are
.IR grouped .
As many T messages as will fit are written at once and their R messages
read back at once, so that opening a thing, reading it and closing it is
one round trip rather than three.
.PP
What is read from a thing is
.IR named .
A read asks for an attribute by string rather than for bytes at an
offset, and the attribute called
.B *
is the list of attributes the thing has.
.SH MESSAGES
.PP
Numbers are little endian.  A string is a two-byte length followed by
that many bytes, not terminated.  Every message begins
.IP
.nf
size[4] type[1] tag[2]
.fi
.PP
and the rest is
.IP
.nf
Tsession	sid[8]		resume that session, or 0 for a new one
Rsession	sid[8]
Topen		fid[4] nfid[4] how[1] name[s]
Ropen		qid[8] version[8] lease[8]
Tread		fid[4] count[4] attr[s]
Rread		version[8] data[s]
Twrite		fid[4] attr[s] data[s]
Rwrite		version[8]
Tclose		fid[4]
Rclose		version[8]
Tlease		fid[4]
Rlease		lease[8]
Tstream		fid[4] period[4] count[4] attr[s]
Rstream		period[8]
Tauth		ticket[s]
Rauth		user[s]
Rerror		ename[s]
.fi
.PP
A container is only opened by whoever it is for; see
.B "WHO IS ASKING"
below.
.PP
.B Topen
walks the name from the root, clones
.I fid
into
.I nfid
and opens it: the walk, the clone and the open of 9P in one message.  A
group begins with
.BR Tsession .
Over
.B #O/pip
the session it settles on is remembered against the connection, so a
client need not name it again in every group; on a wire there is no
connection to remember it against and every group says which session it
is for.
.SH TRANSPORT
.PP
Pip is spoken over
.B #O/pip
by writing a group and reading its replies, and it is spoken on a wire
of its own.
.IP
.nf
echo pipether ether0 on >/n/osprey/ctl
.fi
.PP
takes a conversation on that interface carrying ethernet type
.BR 0x910A ,
and answers every group that arrives in a frame with its replies in a
frame back to whoever sent it.  Nothing about that is a connection:
each group says which session it is for, which is exactly what a session
outliving its connection buys.
.SH WHO IS ASKING
.PP
A container is only opened by whoever it is for.  Over
.B #O/pip
that is the process's own name, which the kernel knows and did not have
to be told.  Over a wire it is nobody: a frame may say anything, so it
says nothing, and a session that has not proved a name may open only the
root.
.PP
.B Tauth
presents a
.I ticket
\- a name, a container and a time, signed under a machine's key; see
.IR osprey (3).
If it checks out and has not run out, the session asks as that name from
then on, for the container the ticket names and no other, and
.B Rauth
says which name that is.  A ticket that does not check out leaves the
session with no name at all.
.PP
The name a session proved outlives the connection along with the rest of
the session, which is the point: a client whose wire went away comes
back, says which session it is, and is still who it was.
.SH STREAMS
.PP
.B Tstream
asks for an attribute to keep arriving at a rate rather than to be asked
for.  Once every
.I period
milliseconds,
.I count
times over \- 0 for no end \- an
.B Rread
is sent carrying the
.BR Tstream 's
own tag, unasked, with no request to match it against.  A second
.B Tstream
on the same fid replaces the first and a period of 0 stops it; so does
the fid's lease running out, or the container going away.
.PP
The rate is fixed when the stream is set up and the frames come at that
rate whatever the client is doing, which is what makes it isochronous
and why it needs the wire: over a file nothing can arrive that was not
read for.  A
.B Tstream
asked for over
.B #O/pip
is answered
.LR "no wire to stream on" .
.SH VERSIONS
.PP
A write does not change what it names.  It changes the version the fid is
making, which nobody else can see: a read through that fid gets what it
wrote, a read through any other gets what is committed.  Closing the fid
commits the version, as a whole; nothing is half applied.
.PP
A version once made is not altered again.  A fid that was opened at one
version and comes to be closed when another has been committed over it is
refused, and what it wrote is thrown away: what it was written against is
not what is there any more.  Two clients that begin from the same version
will not both win.
.PP
.B Ropen
says the version the fid begins from,
.B Rwrite
the version it is making, and
.B Rclose
the version it committed, or zero if it wrote nothing.
.SH LEASES
.PP
A fid and a session are each good for a time, said in milliseconds by
.B Ropen
and by
.BR Rlease .
.B Tlease
renews a fid and the session it belongs to.  What is not renewed goes
away: a fid that has lapsed is no longer a fid, and a client that comes
back to a session it has let lapse is told there is no such session and
must open another.  Renewing a session is what
.B Tsession
does, so a client that keeps coming back keeps it.
.SH NAMES
.PP
The root is
.BR / ,
and its only attribute is
.BR * ,
the list of the containers there are.  A container is named by its
number.  Its attributes are those
.IR osprey (3)
reports:
.BR core ,
.BR state ,
.BR budget ,
.BR used ,
.BR period ,
.BR deadline ,
.BR besteffort ,
.BR periods ,
.BR mode ,
.BR memory ,
.BR memused ,
.BR netbytes ,
.BR netmax ,
.BR packetmax ,
.BR packets ,
.BR requests ,
.BR replies ,
.BR output ,
.BR slice ,
.BR data ,
.B pid
and
.BR version .
Of those,
.BR budget ,
.BR period ,
.BR deadline ,
.BR memory ,
.BR netmax ,
.B packetmax
and
.B mode
may be written; the rest are what the container has done rather than what
it was asked to do.
.SH DEVICES
.PP
A uart a container has been given with
.B uart
is a thing too, walked to by the uart's own name
.RB ( /eia1 ),
opened by whoever the container holding it is for.  Its attributes are
.BR * ,
.BR b ,
the line speed,
.BR d ,
the data, and
.BR name .
Reading
.B d
gives what the uart has received, as much as is there, without waiting;
writing it puts the bytes on the container's output queue, so the driver
task sends them; writing
.B b
is the control message the task answers by setting the speed.  A uart
has no versions: its version is 0 and closing the fid commits nothing.
.SH FILES
.PP
On the microkernel configurations the server personality speaks the
same protocol about its files, over the ring
.RB ( os/osprey/cmd/p9/pipfsrv.c ).
There a name is a path in its name space, opened by
.BR Topen ;
a file's attributes are
.BR * ,
.BR data ,
.BR version ,
.B length
and
.BR name ,
of which
.B data
may be written; the root's
.B *
is the root directory's names.  Versions and leases are as above: a write
makes the version the fid is making and the close commits it, as one
record on the log-structured store, and a fid whose file has been
committed over since it was opened is refused.  There is no wire and no
key there, so
.B Tstream
and
.B Tauth
are refused.
.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
Open a container, read every attribute it has and close it, in one round
trip:
.IP
.nf
pip open 2 /5 read 2 '*' read 2 budget close 2
.fi
.PP
Come back to the same session from another process, with the fid still
open, and commit what was written:
.IP
.nf
pip open 2 /5 write 2 budget 777
pip -s 1 read 2 budget lease 2 close 2
.fi
.PP
The write is nobody else's until that close.
.PP
Ask another machine for a container's used time six times over, half a
second apart, and let it arrive:
.IP
.nf
pip -e open 3 /7 stream 3 500 6 used
.fi
.PP
That machine will not have heard of us, so a container that is somebody's
needs the ticket it signed for us first:
.IP
.nf
pip -e auth 'fred 7 0 f18f505a...' open 1 /7 read 1 budget close 1
.fi
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/osprey/pip.h
.br
.B /os/osprey/devosprey.c
.br
.B /os/osprey/cmd/pip.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR osprey (3)
.SH BUGS
.PP
The things pip names are containers; no file system is served over it.
A machine with no key believes every name it is told, and over a wire
that means a session may open only what is nobody's.  A ticket is a
bearer token: whoever holds it is the name in it.  Taking back the grant
it was made under stops it \- at the next read, write, close or stream as
well as at the next open \- but a ticket for a container's own owner is
stopped by nothing short of changing the machine's key.  A session id is drawn at random rather than
counted up, because a session carries what a ticket proved.
A stream is not scheduled against a reservation: it is sent when it is
due by a kernel process that wakes every five milliseconds, so its
period is kept to about that much and not better.  There are eight
streams for the whole machine.  The wire carries a group in one frame, so a group and its
replies must each fit in 1500 bytes; a group whose replies do not is
answered with one error rather than with as many of them as fit, since
a client counting replies against messages would otherwise match them up
wrongly.  Nothing is retransmitted, and a group in a lost frame is
simply not answered.
.PP
A session is remembered against the connection that last used it, so two
connections working one session at once will confuse each other's
replies.  Eight sessions of sixteen fids are all there are, and a fid
holds at most eight written attributes.  What has lapsed is collected
when somebody asks for a session, when a frame arrives on the wire, and
every few milliseconds while a stream is running \- so a machine that is
being asked nothing keeps what has lapsed until it is asked something.
